Fasciculation or Twitch (Undifferentiated Finding)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Visible subcutaneous rippling, Spontaneous single motor unit contractions, Absence of joint movement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-021
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe anxiety and sleep deprivation secondary to unremitting, widespread muscle twitching.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient in a state of acute panic, unable to sleep due to continuous, visible muscle rippling across multiple limbs, fearing an acute, catastrophic neurological event (e.g., ALS). Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to differentiate benign fasciculation syndrome from acute lower motor neuron failure or severe electrolyte derangement, and to provide immediate clinical reassurance and pharmacological anxiolysis to restore sleep prior to daytime neurological workup.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My muscles are rippling and popping constantly all over my body; I am terrified my nervous system is shutting down."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 48-hour history of acute, severe, widespread muscle fasciculations. Timeline: Noticed an isolated twitch in the left calf two days ago; rapidly progressed to continuous, visible rippling under the skin involving the bilateral calves, thighs, and currently the bilateral triceps. Patient reports an intermittent fluttering sensation without any resulting joint movement. Symptoms are severely exacerbated by the patient's escalating anxiety and lack of sleep. Patient admits to high caffeine intake (6 coffees/day) and recent high stress. Crucially, the patient reports a subjective feeling of weakness but denies any actual inability to perform physical tasks. Denies sensory loss or numbness.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but functionally paralyzed by severe health anxiety and insomnia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute psychological distress; high risk of medication mismanagement in an attempt to self-sedate.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, hyper-vigilant, exhausted, continuously inspecting limbs for twitches.
Physical Exam: Detailed neurological examination reveals 5/5 motor strength in all upper and lower extremity muscle groups, including grip, proximal hip flexion, and distal ankle movements. No objective weakness detected on dynamometry testing (manual resistance). Sensation to light touch, pinprick, and vibration is completely intact globally. Deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetric throughout. Plantar responses are flexor. No sustained clonus. Inspection reveals brief, spontaneous, involuntary contractions of individual motor units visible as fine rippling under the skin in the calves and triceps, without joint movement. No macroscopic muscle atrophy or wasting observed.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty energy drink cans and coffee cups present in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign Fasciculation Syndrome (exacerbated by caffeine and acute anxiety) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) - Ruled out clinically by the absolute preservation of 5/5 objective motor strength, normal reflexes, absence of muscle atrophy, and lack of upper motor neuron signs.

Severe Electrolyte Imbalance (e.g., Hypomagnesemia/Hypokalemia) - Unlikely to present purely with fasciculations without broader systemic signs (arrhythmia, severe cramping), though formal chemistry panel is required.

Radiculopathy - Ruled out by the widespread, non-dermatomal distribution of the twitches and the completely normal sensory and reflex examination.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of acute psychological crisis driven by severe, continuous muscle fasciculations. The critical clinical imperative was to immediately perform a rigorous neurological examination to exclude objective lower motor neuron failure (weakness, atrophy, reflex loss). Upon confirming completely intact objective strength and normal neurology, the diagnosis of a benign, hyper-excitable motor state (likely caffeine/stress-induced) was established. Postponing evaluation would have left the patient in a state of escalating panic and severe sleep deprivation. Urgent after-hours assessment definitively excluded catastrophic neurological collapse, provided immediate, evidence-based reassurance, and safely broke the anxiety-insomnia cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a single dose of Diazepam 5mg STAT to break the acute anxiety/insomnia cycle. Advised absolute cessation of all caffeine and stimulants.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Drink two large glasses of water and immediately go to bed after taking the medication, strictly avoiding looking at or touching the muscles. Consequence: Removal of the visual trigger for panic, allowing the central nervous system to down-regulate and sleep to occur (negative reinforcement).
Action: Replace all caffeinated beverages with decaffeinated herbal tea starting tomorrow. Consequence: Elimination of the chemical trigger causing the nerves to misfire, leading to a noticeable reduction in the fluttering sensations over 48 hours (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days for a comprehensive metabolic panel (magnesium, potassium, calcium, thyroid) to ensure no underlying mild derangement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of actual, measurable weakness (e.g., inability to lift the foot, dropping objects), difficulty swallowing, or slurred speech.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; benign twitches are extremely common and distinctly different from the weakness seen in serious neurological diseases. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The twitches will likely fluctuate over the next week. As your anxiety and caffeine levels drop, the nervous system will settle, and the fasciculations will slowly fade into the background.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you actually lose the physical ability to lift your arm or leg, or if you develop any trouble speaking, chewing, or swallowing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system is over-stimulated from caffeine, stress, and lack of sleep, causing the individual muscle fibers to randomly "misfire" like static electricity. Because your clinical strength and reflexes are perfect, we know the main nerve cables are completely healthy. The medication will force your brain to relax so you can sleep and break the cycle.
Actionable Steps: Stop all caffeine immediately. Tonight, your only job is to sleep. Do not stare at your legs. The twitching cannot harm you; it is just static on the line.

